  "summary": "Distinctive temple architecture from Odisha characterized by towering deul structures and intricate carvings.",
  "description": "Features a beehive-shaped tower (deul) over the sanctum with a square lower structure (jagamohana) and elaborate sculptural decoration. Developed from earlier Nagara traditions with regional adaptations reflecting Odia cultural and religious practices.",
  "culturalContext": "The Kalinga deul — the towering rekha shikhara of Odisha's temples, from Parasurameswara (7th c.) to Lingaraja and Konark's Sun Temple (13th c.) — developed a Nagara idiom of its own: curvilinear towers rising in horizontal pagas, carved chaitya windows, and jagamohana porches in front. Temple-building under the Eastern Ganga kings made Bhubaneswar a city of shrines; Konark's stone wheel-dials remain its signature.",
